{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DkTA5NxK6qiF9LuUWJYbgi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WFWjUeJtR4i6PboGBx31jh","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Research MLE rounds scaling","prompt":"I'm investigating why MLE Rounds in a STARK prover show INVERSE scaling: 114ms at APC 0 (99 AIRs) vs 173ms at APC 300 (623 AIRs), despite 2.3x fewer trace cells at APC 300.\n\nIn the codebase at /home/georg/stark-backend, investigate the MLE rounds implementation:\n\n1. Find the Rust code for MLE sumcheck rounds in `crates/cuda-backend/src/` — look for functions related to `mle_rounds`, `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval`, or `stacked_reduction` MLE.\n2. Understand the per-round loop structure: how many rounds are there? What happens per round?\n3. Previous optimizations already batched MLE kernel launches (see `batched_nondegen_mle_round_kernel` and `batched_interpolate_columns_kernel`). Find what per-AIR or per-round overhead remains.\n4. Look for per-trace or per-AIR operations that could explain the scaling problem: D2H copies, buffer allocations, host-side computations.\n5. Check: are there any sequential loops over AIRs/traces within an MLE round that don't benefit from batching?\n\nFocus on identifying what exactly causes the 59ms gap (173ms - 114ms) between APC 300 and APC 0 in MLE Rounds. Report in under 400 words with specific file paths, line numbers, and the operations that scale with AIR count.","subagent_type":"Explore"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":600,"cache_read_input_tokens":69615,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":600},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2e39847-b30b-4a34-8468-d493bf69eac0","uuid":"0944fe78-7cff-4688-ba04-e53