arify something though—the `calculate_zero_hash` kernel from openvm runs during the initial memory setup phase, but the actual Merkle tree construction in the STARK proof uses `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` from stark-backend, which handles zero-padding natively. Looking at the kernel implementation, it hashes all rows up to the padded power-of-2 height, including the zero rows.\n\nSo that 109ms from `calculate_zero_hash` isn't part of the STARK trace exclusion at all—it's purely in OpenVM's memory system. I should remove it entirely from my STARK analysis and focus on the actual breakdown: LogUp GKR taking 544ms (39%), and Trace Commit at 253ms (18%) with the row hashing kernel dominating at 231ms.\n\nLooking at the remaining components—Round 0 at 237ms, MLE Rounds at 173ms, WHIR at 101ms, and Stacked Reduction at 73ms—I need to figure out which optimizations will actually move the needle at APC 300 without hurting APC 0 performance. Pre-computing Round 0 interaction DAGs during keygen looks promising since the per-AIR overhead from SymbolicDagBuilder is eating up 0.4-0.6ms per AIR, and that compounds across the 8 threads.\n\nThe DAG itself is deterministic and reusable across segments and proofs, so caching it in AirDataGpu follows the pattern already established with the logup buffer preallocation. I'm estimating around 78ms total savings across both segments if I can cache just the DAG construction, though the per-AIR data uploads would still happen per-proof. This feels medium-high probability with moderate complexity.\n\nFor the MLE Rounds overhead, the per-round eq_ub_per_trace loop is iterating 623 times with field arithmetic, and there's also per-round host-to-device transfer happening that could be optimized.\n\nNow looking at the Poseidon2 hashing during trace commit—it's taking 231ms across 25 instances, which breaks down to about 9.2ms per stacked trace. The kernel is hashing all rows including the zero-padded ones, so there's potential to sk