und 114ms, but the actual measurement shows 173ms—there's clearly something else happening. I'm realizing I missed the LogUp-specific kernels that run per window and per trace: precompute_logup_combinations, precompute_lambda_combinations, zerocheck_monomial, and logup_monomial. These are launched separately from the batched kernels and are part of the MLE round computation, so they're adding significant overhead that I haven't accounted for yet.\n\nLooking at the profiler output more carefully, I notice there are actually two different \"MLE Rounds\" components—one in the Constraints phase and one in the Stacked Reduction phase. The 173ms measurement is from the Constraints phase, which likely includes these LogUp Zerocheck operations that are distinct from the Stacked Reduction MLE rounds I was analyzing.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7856,"cache_read_input_tokens":110013,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7856},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2e39847-b30b-4a34-8468-d493bf69eac0","uuid":"f93b4856-d05e-4ec0-8a16-92bdf9aa343c"}
