g at those 40 largest AIRs, the GPU kernel time alone is around 120ms total, with CPU overhead adding another 80ms, putting each segment at roughly 100ms. After optimization, if I can cut the CPU overhead down to 48ms, that brings each segment to about 84ms—a 16ms savings per segment or 32ms total. The other threads would see more dramatic percentage improvements since their CPU overhead dominates, but thread 0 will likely remain the bottleneck even after optimization, so the wall time improvement is probably closer to 32ms overall.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the savings are smaller than I initially thought because the GPU kernel time is the real constraint here. But there's something I overlooked: those large AIRs have much more expensive DAG construction due to their complexity and constraint interactions, so the per-AIR CPU savings might actually be closer to 1.2-1.5ms rather than just 0.8ms. Plus, there's overlap between GPU and CPU work—while one AIR's kernel is running, I can prepare the next one, and the blocking part is only the D2H transfer and subsequent iDFT operations.\n\nFor large AIRs with long kernels (5-13ms), this CPU work gets hidden behind the GPU execution, so caching doesn't help much. But for small AIRs with short kernels (0.05-0.5ms), the CPU overhead sits on the critical path, which is where the caching optimization would actually matter.\n\nSince wall time is determined by the slowest thread, and thread 0 handles the large AIRs where CPU overhead is already hidden, the actual improvement from caching is only 10-15ms per segment — maybe 20-30ms total. That's less impressive than I'd hoped.\n\nBut looking back at the contiguous chunking results, the thread times spread across 26ms (83-109ms), whereas round-robin brought that down to just 7ms (93-100ms). So the question is whether thread 0 was actually the bottleneck to begin with.\n\nWith contiguous chunking, thread 0 hit 109ms per segment while thread 7 was only at 83ms. After applying the caching optimization, thread 0 would drop to around 95ms while thread 7 falls to 50ms, making thread 0 the new bottleneck at 95ms per segment — that's roughly 190ms total per proof compared to the original 237ms.\n\nBut wait, I need to check what dispatching strategy is actually in use right now, since the round-robin approach from the earlier task might already be applied.\n\nLooking at the git log, the round-robin commit is definitely there and wasn't reverted, so Round 0 is currently using round-robin assignment instead of contiguous chunking. That means all threads should be running at similar times around 93-100ms per segment with only a 7ms spread between them. If I divide 237ms across 2 segments by roughly 78 AIRs per thread, I'm getting about 1.24ms per AIR, which doesn't match my earlier 3.0ms estimate — I need to recalculate this properly.\n\nLooking at the historical measurements, the round-robin optimization brought Round 0 down to 277ms, then the prealloc-round0-threshold-buffers optimization got it to 242ms, and now I'm seeing 237ms. So the per-segment time is around 119ms, which with 8 threads gives roughly 3.05ms per AIR — that's more consistent with my earlier calculation.\n\nIf I can shave off 0.8ms per AIR through further optimization, the per-thread time drops to about 88ms, meaning 176ms total per proof. That's a potential 61ms improvement, and since round-robin keeps threads balanced, that savings should translate directly to wall time.\n\nNow looking at the APC 0 case with 99 AIRs running single-threaded across 5 segments at 35ms per segment, I get about 1.75ms per AIR, which is notably lower than the 3.05ms I'm seeing at higher APC values.  The GPU dominates here, so there's much less CPU overhead to optimize—only around 0.25ms per AIR, meaning I can only save about 0.2ms per AIR, totaling roughly 20ms across all 99 AIRs. That brings Round 0 down from 177ms to 157ms.\n\nComparing the net STARK improvements: at APC 300 I'd go from 1387ms to 1326